      "text": "First, we need to find the area of the square. The diagonal of the square is given as 5 inches. We can use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the length of one side of the square. The diagonal divides the square into two right triangles, so we have:\n\n$5^2 = a^2 + a^2$\n$25 = 2a^2$\n$a^2 = \\frac{25}{2}$\n$a = \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{2}} = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n\nThe area of the square is then:\n\n$A_{square} = \\text{side}^2 = \\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25}{2} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}$\n\nNext, we need to find the area of the circle. We are given that the diameter of the circle is 5 inches, so the radius is:\n\n$r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}$\n\nThe formula for the area of a circle is:\n\n$A_{circle} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) = \\frac{25\\pi}{4}$\n\nFinally, we can find the difference in area between the circle and the square:\n\n$A_{circle} - A_{square} = \\frac{25\\pi}{4} - 12.5$\n\nUsing the approximation $\\pi \\approx 3.14$, we get:\n\n$A_{circle} - A_{square} \\approx \\frac{25 \\times 3.14}{4} - 12.5 = 19.625 - 12.5 = 7.125$\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth, we have:\n\n$\\boxed{7.1}$ square inches\n\nThe answer is: 7.1",
