      "text": "To solve the problem, we will first calculate the area of the square and then the area of the circle. We will then subtract the area of the circle from the area of the square to find the difference.\n\n1. Finding the side length of the square:\n\n1. Given: The diagonal of the square is 5 inches.\n2. Let \"s\" represent the side length of the square.\n3. We know that the diagonal (d) of a square with side length (s) is given by the formula: ( d = s * sqrt(2) ).\n4. Manipulating this formula to solve for side length ( s ), we get: ( s = d / sqrt(2) ).\n5. Substituting the provided diagonal to find the side length: ( s = 5 / sqrt(2) ).\n\n2. Calculating the area of the square:\n\n1. The area of the square (A_square) is given by the side length squared: ( A_{text{square}} = s^2 ).\n2. Substituting the calculated side length: ( A_{text{square}} = left(frac{5}{sqrt(2)}right)^2 ).\n\n3. Finding the radius of the circle:\n\n1. Given: The diameter of the circle is 5 inches.\n2. The radius (r) is half the diameter: ( r = frac{diameter}{2} ).\n3. Substituting the provided diameter: ( r = frac{5}{2} ).\n\n4. Calculating the area of the circle:\n\n1. The area of the circle (A_circle) is given by the formula: ( A_{text{circle}} = pi r^2 ).\n2. Substituting the radius: ( A_{text{circle}} = pi left(frac{5}{2}right)^2 ).\n\n5. Determining the difference in areas (A_{text{square}} - A_{text{circle}}):\n\n1. Finally, we subtract the area of the circle from the area of the square: ( A_{text{square}} - A_{text{circle}} ).\n2. Simplifying, this is ( left(frac{5}{sqrt(2)}right)^2 - pi left(frac{5}{2}right)^2 ).\n\nThus, the difference in area is ( left(frac{5}{sqrt(2)}right)^2 - pi left(frac{5}{2}right)^2 ).\n\nThe answer is ( left(frac{5}{sqrt(2)}right)^2 - pi left(frac{5}{2}right)^2 ).",
